X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96075325006466

I asked about scheduling partial backups with NetWorker SingleServer:
> 
> > Fair enough that the fancy stuff like cross network backups won't work, 
> > but all I want to do is to set up a scheduled backup of one machine onto
> > it's own tapedrive. This works, but only if I want to backup the whole 
> > filesystem at one go, which I don't. As far as I can see, there is no way 
> > to set Networker to back up say just /users in a backup schedule. Am I 
> > supposed to buy more licences from DEC to do something this simple?
> 
I got a really clear and complete answer from Paul Rockwell - thanks Paul
(many thanks also to the others who replied - I appreciate your help)
Here is Paul's reply in full:

> The NetWorker SingleServer software will not let you modify the client
> record for the system it's running on - and the save sets that it backs up
> defaults to "All", which means you get all file systems mounted on the system.
> 
> It also won't let you modify schedules.
> 
> To modify schedules and that client record, you need the full NetWorker
> server license (model # QL-2ALA?-AA in the US). However, if you have an
> AlphaServer system shipped after December 1994, you *may* be in luck. At 
> least in the USA, those systems shipped with a Network Application Support (NAS)
> 200 license. After you install it, and load the appropriate software from the
> Layered Products CD-ROM (which is not much more than an installation program),
> that NAS200 license explodes into several layered product licenses. One of 
> which is.....  a NetWorker server license.
> 
> You don't need any NSR client license.
> 
> So, you may already have the license you need... Check it out.
> 
> If all you want to *ever* do with NSR SingleServer is back up your /users,
> you can use NSR directives to force it to skip over the file systems that
> you don't want.
> 
> Simply create a file called .nsr in the top level directory of each of your
> file systems that you don't want to back up. Put the following in that file:
> 
> +skip: *
> 
> NSR should now skip that file system for backup.
