First, let me thank those who responded:

jmh@rto.dec.com (Jan Mark Holzer)
alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow ... )
olle@cb.uu.se (Olle Eriksson)
Knut.Hellebo@nho.hydro.com
Monday Simon <MondayS@logica.com>
Martin Moore <martin@jerry.alf.dec.com>
John Stoffel <john@WPI.EDU>
"Dr. Tom Blinn, 603-881-0646" <tpb@zk3.dec.com>
ron_barrett@corp.Cubic.COM


The problem was that the root filesystem needed to be fsck'ed.  After
the machine was incorrectly shutdown, fsck did not run on /.  Booting
into single-user mode and fsck'ing manually fixed the problem.


Jan Mark Holzer pointed out that /etc/fstab is incorrect on systems
shipped before October 18.  This is why fsck didn't run properly on /.
Here's what he says:

	Incorrect fstab entry fis'd prior to 18-Oct-95:

		/dev/rz0a       / ufs rw 1 0
		/dev/rz0g       /usr ufs rw 1 1

	Correct fstab entry: 

		/dev/rz0a	/ ufs rw 1 1
		/dev/rz0g	/usr ufs rw 1 2


Thanks for the help, folks!


